1️⃣ The biggest misconception

❓ “In an rvalue-specifier (&&) function there will not be the this pointer?”

❌ Incorrect

✔ this ALWAYS exists in any non-static member function
✔ Ref-qualifiers (&, &&) do not remove this



In an lvalue-qualified function
void f() &;


Inside f():

this        // type: A*
*this       // lvalue (A&)

In an rvalue-qualified function
void f() &&;


Inside f():

this        // type: A*
*this       // rvalue (A&&)



/*

so if the this is there then how the && rvalue specifier function have the this cause it will be called with A:: ?? so will the this will have the address of or instance of that rvalue is it ?

Excellent question — this is the exact subtlety that confuses almost everyone the first time.
Let’s answer it precisely and mechanically, no hand-waving.

Short answer (lock this in)

Yes.
Even for an && ref-qualified member function, this points to a real object.
That object is a temporary (rvalue) whose lifetime is still active during the call.

*/